{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.\n\nThe code that sequences the repository's checks carries out the first paragraph.\nA repository-validation function that collects validation errors MUST hold them\nin a parameter or local variable named `errors`, and a function with such a\nvariable is a site below; authoring code that collects errors only to abort is\nnot one. After one of its checks fails, a site MUST still run every later check\nthat does not depend on the failed check's result, including later checks in the\nsame loop iteration, and MUST NOT report one failure twice.\n\n::::{sites} error_accumulators\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nexclude: [src/authoring/inputs.rs, src/authoring/tools.rs]\nrule:\n  kind: function_item\n  has:\n    stopBy: { kind: function_item }\n    any:\n      - kind: parameter\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n      - kind: let_declaration\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n  not:\n    inside:\n      stopBy: end\n      kind: mod_item\n      has: { field: name, regex: \"^tests$\" }\nmatch:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut errors: BTreeSet<String> = BTreeSet::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut errors);\n    }\nno_match:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, discovery_errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut problems = Vec::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut problems);\n    }\n  - |\n    mod tests {\n        fn check(errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\n    }\n```\n::::\n\n::::{violations} early_exits\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nwithin: error_accumulators\nrule:\n  any:\n    - kind: try_expression\n    - kind: return_expression\n    - kind: continue_expression\n    - kind: break_expression\nmatch:\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); continue; }\n            if item.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        if !v.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); return; }\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); break; }\n        }\n        if items.is_empty() { errors.push(\"empty\".into()); }\n    }\nno_match:\n  - |\n    fn parse(v: &Value) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n```\n::::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations and where they are assessed**\n\n- **Sequencer obligations (assessed here).**\n  - Validation continues after a failed check.\n  - Every failed check is reported in one run.\n  - An unparseable file counts as one failed check, and checks that don't depend on it still run.\n  - No failure is reported twice.\n  - The requirement says `src/evidence.rs#fn[inspect_repository_with_progress]` sequences the checks, so tests must cover these.\n- **Per-function obligations (delegated to site reviews).** Each function holding an `errors` variable must run every later independent check, including later checks in the same loop iteration, and must not report one failure twice. Each site's own review covers these, so I did not vet the sites.\n\n**Test mapping**\n\n- **Continue after a failure and report every failure.**\n  - `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks]` has many independent failures in one run. They are bad names and fences, input text elements, definition resolution, a bad hash beside a missing file, and inline and misplaced `{fixme}`. It asserts that every message appears.\n  - `fn[collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors]` makes the same kind of assertions across the evidence-file checks.\n  - `fn[schema_rejects_each_malformed_field_and_input_form]` does so across input forms.\n  - `src/execution_coverage/check.rs#mod[tests]/fn[accepted_exceptions_cannot_waive_invalid_measurement_and_checks_still_collect_errors]` covers the coverage check.\n  - `tests/references.rs#fn[malformed_citation_entries_do_not_hide_later_independent_errors]` and `fn[citation_reviews_are_required_current_and_not_orphaned]` cover citation reviews.\n  - `tests/references.rs#fn[unclosed_fences_still_report_unknown_names]` covers unclosed fences.\n- **Parse failures are one failed check, and independent checks still run.**\n  - `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks]` covers a bad `habeas.toml` (`version = 2`), a malformed `book.toml`, and a malformed evidence file. In the same run it asserts that the unknown-reference error and the missing-evidence error for another requirement still appear.\n  - The same test covers an invalid nextest configuration and an invalid `[coverage]` section.\n  - `tests/commands.rs#fn[status_loads_nearest_workspace_file_without_validating_authoring]` shows that `status` and `info` still report a malformed evidence file next to an invalid workspace file.\n  - `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase]` has an invalid JSON evidence file and an unparseable Rust source together. It asserts both errors, and that discovery continues to retained files.\n  - `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_review_kind_loads_both_formats_and_reports_each_application_failure_once]` covers malformed review records.\n- **No failure reported twice.**\n  - `failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks` sorts and dedups the full error list and asserts nothing was removed.\n  - `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` counts exactly one occurrence of the `habeas.toml` and `book.toml` errors, and checks for no duplicates in the coverage-configuration cases.\n  - `fn[git_index_failure_is_reported_once_and_later_checks_still_run]` asserts one discovery error while later checks still run.\n  - `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs#fn[an_unlistable_review_directory_is_reported_once]` and `src/index.rs#mod[tests]/fn[an_unreadable_record_is_reported_once]` cover unreadable directories and records.\n  - `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[failed_site_queries_do_not_repeat_errors_through_dependent_violations]` covers site queries that fail.\n  - `fn[a_violation_query_needs_a_site_query]` and `fn[within_cannot_name_another_violation_query]` each assert a single finding.\n- **MAY report only the first parse problem.** This imposes nothing to test.\n- **Format of each error as a `validation_error`.** This convention is covered by the requirements for each check, not assessed here.\n\n**Site query `error_accumulators`**\n\nI ran the query by name and scanned its matches. They are about 41 non-test functions with an `errors` parameter or `let errors` binding. Examples are `src/evidence.rs#fn[inspect_repository_with_progress]`, `validate_evidence_file`, `src/citations.rs#fn[validate_review]`, `src/sites.rs` validators, `src/terms.rs`, `src/rust_coverage.rs`, `src/github.rs#fn[add_issue_findings]` (a `BTreeSet`), and `crates/habeas-sdk/src/access.rs#impl[DiscoverySources]/fn[files]`.\n\nI searched for constructs the query could miss:\n- `mut errors` parameters\n- tuple-destructured `errors`\n- closure parameters named `errors`\n- `self.errors` collectors\n- accumulators under other names, such as `discovery_errors` or `problems`\n\nAll non-test hits are already selected. The remaining hits are in `mod tests`, which the query excludes, or in the two excluded authoring files that collect errors only to abort. I found no function the query misses.\n\nTwo selections are borderline but defensible: `src/execution_coverage/book.rs#fn[load_saved]` and `src/index.rs` `load_path`. Both gather errors that feed validation or diagnostic output. I do not count them as out-of-scope.\n\n**Query snippets**\n\n- **Site query.** The match snippets cover the parameter form and the `let` form (with a `BTreeSet`). The no_match snippets cover a different name (`discovery_errors`, `problems`) and a `tests` module.\n- **Violation query `early_exits`.** The match snippets cover `?`, `continue`, `return` and `break`, each followed by a later check. The no_match snippet covers a non-site function that uses `?`. The query does not flag `panic!`, `process::exit` or a `let … else` diverging with a macro. A violation query that misses constructs is not a gap.\n\nNo gaps found.",
  "references": "691fd6bd624b4c8f838b869bac71e20869976911a46320cec082c7f665ec6577",
